---
// Home page FAQ: one <details> per question, so each answer expands in place without any script.
---

<section class="row faq" aria-labelledby="faq-title">
  <h2 class="row-title" id="faq-title">FAQ</h2>
  <div class="faq-list">
    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary>How do I use this?</summary>
      <div class="faq-answer">
        <p>Point your coding agent at this repo and tell it to fuzz your library :)</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary>What kinds of things can I fuzz?</summary>
      <div class="faq-answer">
        <p>The main use case is fuzzing libraries that define an API (both large and small).</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary>How is STITCH++ different from ___?</summary>
      <div class="faq-answer faq-vs-list">
        <div class="faq-vs">
          <h3><span class="faq-vs-tag">vs</span> libFuzzer, AFL++, honggfuzz, ...</h3>
          <p>Those are fuzzing engines which require a harness. Each of them makes different choices about how
            to collect coverage, mutate, keep track of inputs, etc...</p>
          <p>STITCH++ introduces both a fuzzable specification (which replaces a traditional fuzz harness) and a
            new fuzzing engine designed to support this specification.</p>
          <p>The STITCH++ fuzzing engine is built with LibAFL (same authors as AFL++) and can still make use of
            modern fuzzing features, for example sanitizer coverage, compare-logging, forkserver, etc...</p>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-vs">
          <h3><span class="faq-vs-tag">vs</span> FUDGE, FuzzGen, PromptFuzz, PromeFuzz, CKGFuzzer, OGHarn, oss-fuzz-gen, ...</h3>
          <p>Lots of prior work explores the idea of automatically generating fuzz harnesses (both with and
            without LLMs).</p>
          <p>STITCH++ instead creates a fuzzable specification, a replacement of (many) traditional fuzz
            harnesses.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-vs">
          <h3><span class="faq-vs-tag">vs</span> STITCH</h3>
          <p>Work on stitching started in early 2025 and the original tool (which we describe in our paper) was
            built in a LLM-pipeline world, not yet an agent-powered world.</p>
          <p>Many of the design choices we made were necessary at the time for weaker models, and are no longer
            necessary.</p>
          <p>STITCH++ is an agent-forward rebuilt version of STITCH, where we retain only the fuzzing core and we
            extend support to many more languages.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-vs">
          <h3><span class="faq-vs-tag">vs</span> GraphFuzz</h3>
          <p>I'm also the author of GraphFuzz :)</p>
          <p>In a sense, stitching is the LLM-powered successor. It makes possible what used to be very tedious
            to do by hand.</p>
          <p>The key innovation was introducing extrinsic type-state allowing the spec to model open-ended
            constraints. There are also a lot of engineering improvements with STITCH++.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary>How expensive is it?</summary>
      <div class="faq-answer">
        <p>The code is free and open-source; the only cost is writing the specification (most likely using an
          agent), compute to run the fuzzer, and any costs associated with triaging bugs (also probably an
          agent).</p>
        <p>Note that once the spec is created, the fuzzing itself is purely computational, no LLM usage.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary>Will it find false positives?</summary>
      <div class="faq-answer">
        <p>Yes, sort of, but we expect this to improve:</p>
        <p>All the bug reports contain real runnable code, so there is no hallucination risk. The only risk is
          that a bug may be misusing the target API.</p>
        <p>You could also use STITCH++ bug reports as input to some other kind of triage system.</p>
        <p>The fuzzing engine accurately respects the specification. So if your specification perfectly
          defines the expected way to use the API, you will be fine.</p>
        <p>In practice, for any non-trivial API, it's basically impossible to produce an accurate specification
          (let alone understand what the API <em>should</em> do in certain edge cases), even for an expert
          human.</p>
        <p>In practice, what we observed with STITCH (the predecessor) is that most API-misuse bugs are
          "obvious in retrospect" &mdash; that is, while it may be hard to spot a slightly broken specification
          ahead of time, it is generally very obvious if a specific testcase is abusing the API in a way that is
          clearly unintended. Thus, after seeing such a testcase, one can go back and fix the specification to
          avoid it.</p>
        <p>We expect specification development to undergo this sort of refinement loop via dynamically testing
          the specification. In practice, agents are already really good at this and we expect them to continue
          to get better.</p>
      </div>
    </details>
  </div>
</section>
